\DOC LOCAL_AMONTSQR_SM2_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_AMONTSQR_SM2_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across one inlined
almost-Montgomery-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current state
variable by a fresh successor state and adding assumptions characterising the
squared result stored in memory.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes an {int} selecting which code block/offset of the enclosing
program the step corresponds to, and a {string list} naming the result variable
and the input operand variable, which appear in the added assumptions about the
computed value and the memory location it is written to. Applied to a goal
whose conclusion is an {eventually} property of a state {s}, it yields a single
subgoal with the same {eventually} property applied to a fresh later state
variable {s'}:
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   =================================  LOCAL_AMONTSQR_SM2_TAC n ["result"; "input"]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is obtained as follows. All state-dependent assumptions of
{A} — program-counter, register, memory-content and loading facts, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to the current one —
are discharged and re-added with {s'} in place of {s}, the {MAYCHANGE} frame
being extended by the memory region written in this step; purely arithmetic
assumptions are kept unchanged. Three new assumptions appear: the new program
counter value, a bound of the form {t < u} on the result variable, and a
congruence stating that the result is the Montgomery square of the named input
modulo the field prime, together with an equation reading the result out of the
appropriate memory block of the stack frame.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour, on
goals of this one {eventually} shape.

\ENDDOC
